# Stylesheet Ecology
#
# A stylesheet is a standalone document, not a fragment of a bundle. It should
# be readable, disposable, and probeable on its own — and it should say what it
# senses. This names the practice the tree already follows, measures where it
# lapses, and states the one place the ecology genuinely fails: everything
# arrives at once.

#>stylesheet_ecology
#:convention #!css #!architecture #!progressive-enhancement
#:operation #!align
#:fixity #!tending
#:layer #!pragmatics

@css_instruction: ~"./css-instruction.spw"
@component_packing: ~"./component-packing.spw"
@coloring_depth: ~"./coloring-depth.spw"
@attribute_governance: ~"./data-spw-attribute-governance.spw"
@arrival_electrostatics: ~"./arrival-electrostatics.spw"
@manifest: ~"../../public/css/style-core.css"
@payload_report: ~"../../scripts/css-payload-report.mjs"
@breakpoints: ~"../../scripts/normalize-breakpoints.mjs"
@image_theming: ~"../../public/css/systems/image-theming.css"
@paper_motif: ~"../../public/css/systems/paper-motif.css"

operation = "align"
fixity = "tending"

^"thesis"{
  claim: "A stylesheet earns its place by being legible alone. Opened cold, it should answer three questions without reference to any other file: what surface am I dressing, what state do I sense, and how would you prove I am doing it. The tree already believes this — 151 of 155 authored sheets open with a banner that answers at least the first — so this convention defends an existing practice rather than importing one."
  refuse: "Do not let a stylesheet become a fragment that only makes sense once bundled. If a rule cannot be explained without naming three other files, the cluster is in the wrong document."
}

^"file_form"{
  banner: "`/* ===` … `=== */` at the head. 151 of 155 files use it; 1 uses a `---` dialect and 3 have neither. The dialect is not decoration — it is how a reader knows the file declares itself rather than starting mid-thought."
  reads_as: "One line naming the surface and the state it dresses."
  was: "Provenance when a file was split from another, with the date. Splits lose their reason within a month otherwise."
  genome: "`Genome — states sensed:` the `data-spw-*` attributes the file selects on, and `custom properties defined:` a count. Only 18 of 155 carry this today; it is the richest form and the least propagated."
  probe: "How to see the file working — a DevTools toggle, a debug attribute, a route to open. A stylesheet with no probe is a claim with no falsification."
}

^"linguistic_register"{
  claim: "CSS here is written as language, not as configuration. Selectors are phrases; a rule cluster is a paragraph; the file is an essay about one surface."
  kinship: "The sentence is the combinator. `>` names a child, `+` the next sibling, ` ` a descendant, `:has()` a parent that knows what it holds. That is the worldbuilding. A banner that restates a 'law' is not."
  nesting: "Nest a family when the subject is one kinship — a sequence and its next siblings, a ladder and its children — so the reader meets the relationship as structure, not as a repeated parent prefix."
  pseudo_selectors: "`:where()` for zero-specificity defaults so packs and handles can override without an arms race; `:has()` where a parent's dressing genuinely depends on its contents (@arrival_electrostatics and paper-motif both rely on this). Specificity is a cost, not a tool."
  naming: "Class names describe the thing, `data-spw-*` describes its state. When a class encodes state it has taken a job that belongs to an attribute — see @attribute_governance."
}

^"cascade_vs_delivery"{
  claim: "@layer decides which rule wins. It does not decide which file is requested, when it is requested, or how it is cached."
  refuse: "Do not treat the cascade layer list as a load-optimization technique. That misconception keeps spend in core because 'it already lives in effects/ornament'."
  delivery: "Core vs deferred is a request schedule. Flourish spend (wonder, grain, cinematic, ornament) stays in the same layers and arrives through effects/flourish-pack.css after interactive."
  late_link: "A late <link> that omits the layer list is unlayered and beats the stack. flourish-pack restates the geology so idle arrival is restyle, not a cascade coup."
  visitor: "The schedule is for the person, not the cascade. First paint is already a complete page. Idle flourish may only make that page more itself. A shift that hides or shoves what they already read will make them angry."
}

^"progressive_enhancement"{
  claim: "This is where the ecology actually fails, and no amount of file hygiene fixes it."
  measured: "core.css is 1614KB and every visitor downloads it on every route. @payload_report states the finding plainly: 'Core dominates (~1.5 MiB). Route scoping mainly drops other-route CSS (~0.4-0.6 MiB).' Route scoping trims the tail; the trunk is untouched."
  weight: #[
    `tokens/core.css 79KB`,
    `grammar/syntax.css 77KB`,
    `shell/chrome/navigation.css 60KB`,
    `components/runtime-states.css 56KB`,
    `shell/chrome/adaptive.css 51KB`,
    `ornament/ornament.css 47KB`
  ][reg=set]
  rule: "A sheet belongs in core only if a first paint without it is wrong rather than merely plainer. Ornament, wonder, cinematic and metaphysical-paper dress surfaces that are already legible; they are enhancement by their own descriptions and arrive as if they were structure."
  precedent: "themes/packs.css was pulled from core for exactly this reason and is appended on demand. Flourish spend now follows the same delivery cut: tokens stay in core, spend sheets idle-load through flourish-pack.css."
}

^"theme_reach"{
  claim: "A theme pack that moves every colour token and stops at the image boundary is a palette with a hole in it. Seven packs shipped that way — grepping themes/packs.css for `filter`, `saturate` or `image` returned nothing — so a `banked-ember` visitor got the same bright warm-paper plate `neutral-paper` shows, burning out of a dim page."
  contract: "systems/image-theming.css owns the tokens; packs set them. `--spw-image-brightness`, `--spw-image-contrast`, `--spw-image-saturation`, `--spw-image-tint`, `--spw-image-tint-strength`, `--spw-image-edge`. Every default is identity, so a pack that sets nothing changes nothing and an unthemed image looks exactly as it did."
  tint_rule: "Tint lands through a `color-mix` overlay, never `hue-rotate`. Hue-rotate mangles the warm amber points these renders are built around while leaving the teal fields alone; mixing toward a hue keeps relative warmth intact."
  materials: "systems/paper-motif.css places the papergami studies as substrate, one motif per `data-spw-page-family`, so variety comes from what a page already declares rather than from rotation. Packs drive its opacity and blend — paper packs multiply, dark packs screen, because multiply against a dark field erases the plate."
  mobile_rule: "A treatment that switches itself off below a breakpoint has not been made responsive, it has been made desktop-only. The motif changes role on narrow viewports — beside the column becomes beneath it — rather than disappearing."
  capture_rule: "Both systems go identity under a non-default `data-spw-capture-mode`. A screenshot read by another model should carry the image, not this site's mood applied to it."
}

^"breakpoints"{
  ladder: "Rungs are canonical only where usage already clustered — see @breakpoints. 58 distinct thresholds became 32 by snapping drift (719/720/721, 979/980/981, 760 beside 768 beside 48rem) with no move beyond 5%."
  units: "rem, so a reader's text-size preference moves the layout with them. px media queries ignore it."
  posture: "155 of 189 queries were `max-width`. Desktop-first means mobile is whatever survives subtraction, which is why it reads as unfinished. New work states `min-width`."
  open: "Collapsing 16 rungs to a handful of semantic tiers moves layout and wants eyes on real pages. Not a codemod."
}

^"audit"{
  probe_payload: `npm run css:payload — per-route bundle weight and what each route drops`
  probe_breakpoints: `node scripts/normalize-breakpoints.mjs — dry run; reports drift without writing`
  probe_banner: `for f in $(find public/css -name '*.css' -not -path '*/bundles/*'); do head -6 "$f" | grep -q '===' || echo "$f"; done`
  probe_nesting: `find public/css -name '*.css' -not -path '*/bundles/*' -exec grep -l '^\s*&' {} \; | wc -l`
  falsification: #[
    `A sheet enters core that the page reads correctly without.`,
    `A new stylesheet ships with no banner, or a banner with no probe.`,
    `A width media query is authored in px, or at a threshold off the ladder.`
  ][reg=set]
}

#>wonder_stylesheet_ecology_1
?["Can a stylesheet declare its own arrival tier, or must the manifest decide?"]{
  #:depth #!computational ~#lens(progressive enhancement)
  ~#tension: "core membership is decided in style-core.css, a manifest a sheet cannot see. So a file that knows perfectly well it is ornament has no way to say so, and the only record of that judgment is an import line in another document."
  ~#hypothesis: "the banner is already the right place — a `Tier:` line naming core | route | deferred would let the manifest be generated from the sheets rather than maintained against them."
  !probe{ "add a Tier line to the six heaviest sheets, then try to generate style-core.css from the banners. if the generated manifest differs from the authored one, each difference is a membership nobody would defend out loud." }
  $%[sheet.tier, bundle.coreBytes, route.droppedBytes]
}

^"validation"{
  probe: `npm run css:payload && node scripts/normalize-breakpoints.mjs`
  next: "Evict one enhancement sheet from core following the packs.css precedent, and measure. One eviction with a number beats a plan for six."
}
